 "conclusion": "On one 8 GB RTX 3070 boot, two and three concurrent production fits summed to 0.96 to 0.97 of the single-fit sampler throughput because processes time-slice the GPU, so DEFAULT_FITS_PER_GPU stays 1. The JAX working set of one production fit is 0.8 to 1.0 GiB (peak_bytes_in_use) while the default 75 percent preallocation reserves 5,880 MiB. XLA_CLIENT_MEM_FRACTION=0.14 (1,098 MiB pool) ran at full speed inside 1,481 MiB of GPU memory; 0.10 (819 MiB pool) failed at compile time in Triton GEMM autotuning; XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_PREALLOCATE=false ran at full speed inside 2,469 MiB. A smaller pool changes the autotuned kernel choices, which shifts the run by one outer sampler iteration (1,183,000 against 1,189,500 likelihood calls) with ln Z agreeing within one sigma. Three fits at 0.14 fit in 4,406 MiB and were no faster than three fits at 0.28. Lower allocation makes N=2 and N=3 fit with room to spare but does not make them worthwhile; GPU memory was never the constraint.",
